## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about scanning a 128-bit value with fixed, branch-reduced steps and no loops. It exercises bitwise practice and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-width binary search over bit positions
- Handling x==0 gracefully
- Portability across 128-bit types

## Code Example
```c
// Conceptual outline (branch-free version would use masks)
int msbIndex128(uint128 x) {
    if (x == 0) return -1; // illustrative; actual solution avoids branches
    int idx = 0;
    if (x >> 64) { x >>= 64; idx += 64; }
    if (x >> 32) { x >>= 32; idx += 32; }
    if (x >> 16) { x >>= 16; idx += 16; }
    if (x >> 8)  { x >>= 8;  idx += 8; }
    if (x >> 4)  { x >>= 4;  idx += 4; }
    if (x >> 2)  { x >>= 2;  idx += 2; }
    if (x >> 1)  { idx += 1; }
    return idx;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would the approach adapt to 256-bit values?
- How would you verify correctness with randomized tests?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about cross-boundary 128-bit rotations without a native 128-bit type. Exercises correct handling of edge cases like r=0, r=64, and r=127, plus understanding how two 64-bit halves interact during rotation.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit numbers split into hi/lo halves
- Boundary-safe shifts across 64-bit parts
- Distinguishing r<64 vs r>=64 paths and their swap behavior

## Code Example

```c
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned r) {
  r &= 127;
  if (r == 0) return x;
  if (r < 64) {
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi << r) | (x.lo >> (64 - r));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo << r) | (x.hi >> (64 - r));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  } else {
    unsigned t = r - 64;
    uint64_t hi = (x.lo << t) | (x.hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo >> (64 - t)) | (x.hi << t);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement rotateRight128? 
- How do you unit-test edge cases (0, 64, 127) efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses branch-free 128-bit rotation, a common primitive in crypto and low-level optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values via two 64-bit halves
- Branchless selection with masks
- Cross-half rotation logic and edge cases (n=0, n=64, n=127)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, uint8_t n) {
  uint64_t n0 = n & 63;
  uint64_t m  = -((int)((n >> 6) & 1)); // 0 or -1
  uint64_t hi1 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo1 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t hi2 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo2 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = (hi1 & ~m) | (hi2 & m);
  r.lo = (lo1 & ~m) | (lo2 & m);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How might you test for constant-time properties across inputs?
- How would you extend to a constant-time generic rotate for arbitrary bit-widths?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-level data packing and word-level efficiency.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (dilating)
- Inline masks and shifts for 32-bit results
- Morton codes for spatial indexing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t part1by1(uint16_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x &= 0x0000FFFF;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t morton2D(uint16_t x, uint16_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 3D Morton codes (x,y,z) and 64-bit result? 
- How would you adapt for non-power-of-two bit widths or streaming data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of low-level bit hacks: packing, masking, and fixed-time transformations without control flow.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (Z-order) interleaving
- Bit spreading via staged masks
- Fixed, branchless implementation

## Code Example
```cpp
uint32_t spread16(uint16_t v){
  uint32_t x = v;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t interleave16(uint16_t a, uint16_t b){
  return spread16(a) | (spread16(b) << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to interleave 32-bit values into a 64-bit result?
- What tests would you add to validate edge cases like a or b == 0xFFFF?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to manipulate a 128-bit value by composing 64-bit operations, enforcing a non-loop, fixed-sequence approach for performance.

## Key Concepts
- Endianness and byte-level reversal
- Bit-twiddling for 64-bit reversal
- Combining two 64-bit halves into a 128-bit result

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t v) {
  v = ((v & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((v >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  v = ((v & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((v >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  v = (v << 32) | (v >> 32);
  return v;
}
uint128 swapEndian128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if only a single 128-bit type isn’t available?
- How would you validate correctness with representative test patterns (all zeros, all ones, alternating bits)?

## Why This Is Asked
Test ability to count bits with a constant-time sequence of masks and shifts, no loops.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting
- Fixed masks: 0x5555..., 0x3333..., 0x0F0F...
- No branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function popcount64(x) {
  // x is a BigInt
  x = x - ((x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333n) + ((x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n);
  x = (x + (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = x + (x >> 8n);
  x = x + (x >> 16n);
  x = x + (x >> 32n);
  return Number(x & 0x7Fn);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 128-bit using the same pattern?
- What are the performance implications on 32-bit vs 64-bit targets?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests advanced bit manipulation skills. A palindrome check requires full 128-bit reversal and a single branchless comparison. It assesses understanding of endianness, cross-lane bit operations, and how to compose a complex operation from a reusable primitive.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit bit reversal (SWAR-style steps)
- Branchless comparison via bitwise XOR
- Endian-agnostic bit ordering across two 64-bit lanes

## Code Example
```c
int isBitPalindrome128(uint128 x) {
    uint128 r = reverseBits128(x);
    uint128 d = x ^ r;
    return (d == 0) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

In high-throughput diffing pipelines, diffusion-boosting byte-wise bit reversal can be done with a fixed, branchless sequence, testing mastery of multi-stage bit manipulations.

## Key Concepts

- Parallel bit-twiddling across 8-byte lanes
- Masks: 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F, 0x3333333333333333, 0x5555555555555555
- Branchless, constant-time operations

## Code Example

```javascript
function reverseBitsInBytes64(n) {
  n = ((n >>> 4n) | (n << 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  n = ((n >>> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n);
  n = ((n >>> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n);
  return n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test edge cases (0, all ones, alternating patterns)?
- How does endianness affect results across platforms?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests deep bit-slicing, 128-bit composition, and Morton (Z-order) interleaving in a branchless, portable way.

## Key Concepts
- Morton order interleaving
- Bit-slicing with masks and shifts (part1by1 technique)
- 64-bit to 128-bit composition without branches
- Endianness implications for MSB-first construction

## Code Example
```c
struct uint128_t {
    uint64_t hi, lo;
};

// Spread single bits to double-bit positions
static inline uint64_t part1by1(uint64_t x) {
    x = (x ^ (x << 16)) & 0x0000ffff0000ffff;
    x = (x ^ (x << 8))  & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ff;
    x = (x ^ (x << 4))  & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0f;
    x = (x ^ (x << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333;
    x = (x ^ (x << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555;
    return x;
}

struct uint128_t interleave64(uint64_t a, uint64_t b) {
    uint64_t a_spread = part1by1(a);
    uint64_t b_spread = part1by1(b);

    struct uint128_t result;
    result.lo = (a_spread << 1) | b_spread;
    result.hi = (a_spread >> 63) | (b_spread >> 63);
    return result;
}
```

This implementation uses the classic part1by1 technique to spread bits, then combines them with simple shifts and OR operations, achieving the required bit alternation without loops or conditionals.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests mastery of a classic bit-twiddling pattern to find the next permutation of a fixed-popcount bitset in constant time, without loops or branches. It also probes edge cases like overflow and zero input.

## Key Concepts

- Snoob algorithm for next higher with same popcount
- 64-bit overflow behavior and edge cases (n == 0 or max with k ones)
- Pure bitwise/shifts approach, no loops

## Code Example

```c
uint64_t nextWithSamePopcount(uint64_t n) {
  uint64_t smallest = n & -n;
  uint64_t ripple = n + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = n ^ ripple;
  return ripple | ((ones >> 2) / smallest);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for arbitrary word size or signed integers?
- What are the performance implications on different architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates mastery of fixed-popcount permutations. Tests carry propagation and ability to explain a one-shot solution without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Snoob algorithm for next with same popcount
- Carry ripple and bitwise masks
- Edge-case handling for zero or maximal permutations

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t nextBitPermutation64(uint64_t x) {
    // Assumes x != 0 and not the maximal permutation for its popcount
    uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
    uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
    uint64_t ones = x ^ ripple;
    ones = (ones >> 2) / smallest;
    return ripple | ones;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to arbitrary bit-widths?
- Compare this approach to a loop-based generator in terms of performance and readability.

## Why This Is Asked
Advanced bit manipulation questions assess knowledge of branchless arithmetic and de Bruijn indexing techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Bit manipulation: isolating the least significant bit using two's complement arithmetic
- De Bruijn sequence mapping for constant-time position lookup
- Branchless, constant-time operations
- Proper edge-case handling for n == 0

## Code Example
```c
int ctz64(uint64_t n) {
  // Implementation uses de Bruijn indexing
  uint64_t t = n & -n;
  // table[index] yields trailing-zero count
  return table[(t * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a01ULL) >> 58];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of fast, branchless bit tricks and constant-time bit-scanning utilities common in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- Isolating least-significant 1: v = n & -n
- De Bruijn-based indexing: map a 5-bit index to a trailing-zero count
- Handling edge case n == 0 (return 32)
- Static lookup table of 32 entries

## Code Example
```c
uint32_t countTrailingZeros32(uint32_t n){
  static const int debruijn[32] = {0,1,28,2,29,14,24,3,30,22,20,15,25,17,4,8,31,27,13,23,21,19,16,7,26,12,18,9,6,11,5,10};
  if(n == 0) return 32;
  uint32_t v = n & -n;
  return debruijn[(v * 0x077CB531) >> 27];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit inputs?
- What are alternatives that avoid a static table, e.g., using binary search on bit groups?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of per-byte bit-twiddling, branchless design, and parallel bit tricks that map well to performance-critical code paths in systems programming.

## Key Concepts
- Byte-wide parity, parallel bit tricks, constant-time operations
- Masking to extract per-byte indicators
- No branches or loops

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t perByteParity64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t y = x;
  y ^= y >> 1;
  y ^= y >> 2;
  y ^= y >> 4;
  return y & 0x0101010101010101ULL;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 32-bit blocks? 
- Can you adapt to compute even parity per byte instead of odd parity?

## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates capability to manipulate multi-word integers with pure bit tricks, a common requirement in crypto and low-level systems programming. It tests cross-lane thinking and branch-free implementation.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-lane bit propagation between hi and lo.
- Branch-free 128-bit rotation using only shifts and OR.

## Code Example

```c
uint128 rotateLeft1281(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = (x.lo << 1) | (x.hi >> 63);
  r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to rotate left by k (0 <= k < 128) without branches?
- How would you test boundary cases like k=0, k=127, across hi/lo boundaries?
